
✅ 1. Better Performance

Asynchronous Logging in Log4j2 uses LMAX Disruptor, which is extremely fast and designed for high-throughput, low-latency systems.
Logback also supports async logging, but it relies on standard Java queues, which are slower compared to Disruptor.


✅ 2. Garbage-Free Logging

Log4j2 is designed to minimize object creation during logging, reducing GC pauses.
Logback creates more temporary objects, which can lead to higher garbage collection overhead in high-load environments.


✅ 3. More Flexible Configuration

Log4j2 supports XML, JSON, YAML, and Properties configuration formats.
Logback primarily supports XML (and Groovy optionally), making Log4j2 more versatile.


✅ 4. Advanced Filtering

Log4j2 allows filters at multiple levels: context, logger, and appender.
Logback has filters but they are less granular compared to Log4j2.


✅ 5. Plugin Architecture

Log4j2 is highly extensible with a plugin system for custom appenders, layouts, and filters.
Logback is extensible but lacks a formal plugin architecture.


✅ 6. Dynamic Configuration Reload

Log4j2 can automatically reload configuration files without restarting the application.
Logback supports reloading but is less robust and requires additional setup.


✅ 7. Built-in Support for Async Context Data

Log4j2 provides Thread Context Map (MDC) and Thread Context Stack (NDC) with async support.
Logback supports MDC but async handling is less optimized.


✅ 8. Lookups

Log4j2 supports Lookups for dynamic values (e.g., environment variables, system properties).
Logback does not have an equivalent feature.


✅ 9. Performance Benchmarks

In most benchmarks, Log4j2 outperforms Logback, especially in asynchronous mode.

